WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:06.560
- Thank you so much for joining us

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.530
for a discussion on the
conflicts and synergies

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:13.290
between poverty alleviation
and climate change mitigation.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.950
This is the fourth in a series,

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:16.430
the " Yale Development Dialogues

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:18.610
Economic Policy Lessons from History."

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.660
I'm Catherine Cheney, and
I'll be your moderator today.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.350
So poverty reduction and
environmental protection

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:26.610
are often seen as conflicting goals.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.960
But the climate crisis
is forcing many of us

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.490
to rethink economic growth.

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:33.100
So the question we'll be asking today

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.430
is what might that look like?

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.210
What are green pathways out of poverty?

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.970
And how might we redefine
international development

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:42.160
in a way that does not
present climate progress

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.190
and human progress as incompatible.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:46.740
Joining us to discuss this topic,

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.980
I'm thrilled to welcome, Sunil Amrith,

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.350
the Renu and Anand Dhawan
Professor of History at Yale,

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.210
and current chair of the
South Asian Studies Council.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:58.400
His research focuses on histories
of environment, migration,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.770
and public health, across
South and Southeast Asia.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.740
Julie Livingston is Silver Professor

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.860
of Social and Cultural
Analysis and History

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:07.390
at New York University,

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.280
and author of " Self-Devouring Growth:

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.970
a Planetary Parable as
Told from Southern Africa,"

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.650
which you'll hear more about.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.570
Her work is at the
intersection of history,

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:17.770
anthropology and public health.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.470
Rory Stewart is a Senior Fellow

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.760
at the Yale Jackson
Institute for Global Affairs

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.708
where he focuses on contemporary politics

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:26.550
in crisis and on international development

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.112
and intervention in fragile
and conflict affected states

00:01:29.112 --> 00:01:31.970
and very relevant to today's conversation.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:34.490
Stewart also served as
the UK Secretary of State

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:35.880
for International Development,

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.780
where he doubled the UK investment

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:39.760
in international climate and environment.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.550
And faced some challenges in doing so

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.630
which I know I'm looking
forward to hearing more about.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:46.610
And finally, Rohini Pande,

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.860
is the Henry J. Heinz II
Professor of Economics

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.500
and Director of the Economic
Growth Center at Yale.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.810
Her work focuses on
testing innovative ways

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.836
to make the state more
accountable to it's citizens,

00:01:56.836 --> 00:01:58.810
such as strengthening women's economic

00:01:58.810 --> 00:01:59.910
and political opportunities,

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.510
ensuring that environmental regulations

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.050
reduce harmful emissions,

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.830
and providing citizens effective means

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.320
to voice their demands for state services.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.150
So I'd love to open this
conversation with Sunil.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:13.890
And Sunil you really championed this topic

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.230
as one we should feature in
the Yale Development Dialogue.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.802
So I wonder if you can kind
of set the scene for us.

00:02:19.802 --> 00:02:22.601
What does it look like and
what are some of the challenges

00:02:22.601 --> 00:02:25.780
in terms of this perceived trade-off?

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.030
And can you take us
through some of the history

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:29.780
of environmental protection

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.480
and development being seen as a trade-off?

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.340
- Thank you Catherine.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.090
And thanks everyone for joining us.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.220
This was really brought
home to me just last month

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.480
when we had the collapse or
partial collapse of two dams

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.453
in Uttarakhand Northern India.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.220
Many people were killed most
of them workers on the site,

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.660
and I was struck that
from more than a decade.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:52.700
Environmentalist's in the courts,

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:53.800
have been warning,

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.650
that that sort of
infrastructural development

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:59.290
in such an ecologically
fragile region is reckless.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:03.050
That a catastrophe like
this was almost inevitable.

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:05.340
And yet these projects plowed on.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.700
And one of the first
responses to the disaster

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.560
by the Chief Minister of Uttarakhand,

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.300
was to very quickly point out,

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.070
that we shouldn't let what has happened

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:19.740
lead people to adopt an
anti-development narrative.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.810
And that really gave me pause,

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:25.130
because I realized in a sense
how pervasive that view is.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:29.820
The idea that environmental
consent is anti development.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.350
And as a historian I'm very curious

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:36.100
and concerned with where that
pattern of thinking came from.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.800
And I'd like to maybe just
give you two or three examples

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.330
'cause I think we need to
go back to the 1960s, 1970s

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.180
to really understand it.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.230
The first example is Rachel
Carson and " Silent Spring."

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.330
Carson is rightly celebrated

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:51.550
I think as a scientific
hero of the 20th century.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:53.130
We all owe a huge amount

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.810
to her courage and her intelligence.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.560
But at the time and many in
the development community

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.410
felt like the rug had been
pulled from under them.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.646
And the link of course was DDT.

00:04:03.646 --> 00:04:05.970
Rachel Carson's " Silent Spring"

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:09.390
was one among a few factors
that led the US government

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.460
to withdraw it's massive funding

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.000
for the WHO's malaria eradication program

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.360
and to divert a lot of
that to population control.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.250
Now the WH)'s program
was already in trouble

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:20.550
by the early 1960s.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:24.070
There was a lot of evidence
of insecticide resistance,

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:25.670
but still there was a feeling

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:28.170
that this transformative
life saving technology

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:29.383
had been taken away.

00:04:30.218 --> 00:04:33.080
I'll give you a second example
from about a decade later

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.321
early 1970s.

00:04:34.321 --> 00:04:37.390
The Club of Rome's report,
"the Limits to Growth."

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:39.100
And in many ways that is a forerunner

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:41.040
of so many of the debates we're having now

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.240
about growth and the environment.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.930
But one of the main concerns
of the Club of Rome report

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.990
was with population
growth in poor countries.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:53.080
And immediately the response
from a lot of poor countries

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.200
was that the problem is
not population growth,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.660
the problem is inequality
and over consumption.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.620
And I think that one of the
most forceful statements

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:01.453
of that view

00:05:01.453 --> 00:05:03.440
comes from India's Prime
Minister Indira Gandhi,

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:04.990
whose one of the few heads of state

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:07.920
to attend the first UN conference
on the global environment

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.030
in Stockholm in 1972.

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.410
And her speech there I
think still has quite a lot

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:13.440
of moral power to it.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:14.470
Because the first thing she did

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:16.920
was to acknowledge environmental crisis.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:20.530
But the second thing she did
was to say that it has to wait

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:22.290
for poverty reduction.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.940
That poverty and her famous
line from that speech

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:27.800
is not poverty the greatest polluter.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.690
And she added that the poorest countries

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:32.110
and perhaps the poorest
people within those countries

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:33.760
suffered from problems

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.125
that they were perhaps least
responsible at full causing.

00:05:37.125 --> 00:05:40.000
Finally, I think through the 80s, and 90s,

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.663
you see a backlash against
animal conservation projects

00:05:43.663 --> 00:05:45.710
that tended to treat local people

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:48.060
often very marginalized
people, indigenous people

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:49.333
as the enemy.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:52.560
I think we need to
understand this history.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.970
I think we need to
recognize the moral force

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:56.619
behind some of these arguments,

00:05:56.619 --> 00:05:59.710
in order perhaps to move beyond them.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.070
For the simple reason
that I think that today

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:03.723
and certainly in the medium term,

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.560
climate change and a whole range

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.550
of other environmental crises,

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.870
present perhaps the most
serious obstacle of all

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.460
to poverty reduction and
to large numbers of people

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:15.543
being able to live lives of dignity.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:18.443
- Thank you Sunil

00:06:18.443 --> 00:06:19.990
and I think that's a powerful articulation

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:21.620
for the importance of these conversations

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:22.950
needing to understand this history

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:24.810
in order to tackle these challenges.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:26.870
And Julie, I see you nodding your head

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:28.160
it seems like you've come across

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:30.310
many of these dynamics in your research,

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:33.070
on the African continent
and specifically in Botswana

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:34.610
which I know is a focus of your book.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:36.470
So can you tell us a little bit more

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:39.710
about, what I know you've
described as the consequences

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:41.340
of a growth at all costs model

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.853
and what lessons we might
learn from Botswana?

00:06:45.357 --> 00:06:48.313
- Absolutely. Thank you Catherine.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:54.010
You know Botswana for several decades

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.180
after it's independence from Great Britain

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:59.040
had one of the fastest growing
economies in the world.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.620
It's had a really well-managed
development trajectory.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:06.260
The standard of living has risen.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:09.780
There are important
social welfare programs,

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:12.300
a functioning public health system,

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:16.003
participatory democracy, and yet,

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:20.530
climate change, is helping us realize

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:24.150
that even in a best case
development scenario

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:29.150
which most places cannot
realize, it's not sustainable.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.590
There needs to be a
radically different thinking

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.840
about how this is going to work.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.603
Botswana is currently
mining it's groundwater,

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.500
aspects of climate change

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:46.173
are contributing to desertification.

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:50.040
This is a place that
had been a carbon sink,

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:54.890
and yet nonetheless, is going
through a really drastic

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:57.300
kind of terraforming,

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.860
that threatens it's longterm wellbeing.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:05.720
And I think, that raises
all kinds of questions.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:06.610
First of all,

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:10.300
we have to recognize
that carbon is important

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:13.760
but it's not the only problem.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:17.380
We have a whole set of
interlinked problems.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:20.930
Toxicity and pollution,
the problem of waste,

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:24.380
loss of bio diversity, top soil erosion,

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.733
and they fit together into a web,

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:33.850
that is synergistic and
extremely threatening

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:39.690
particularly to people who
are already living in poverty.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:43.180
When you start looking at it closely,

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:47.100
what you realize is that
you can't really separate

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.710
the problem into that of nation states

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:52.690
in the way that development
usually functions.

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:55.360
Botswana doesn't have
the ability to control

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:56.800
it's own climate.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:00.350
It's not the major producer
of carbon in the world,

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:03.220
even though it is connected
in a global system

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:05.328
of consumption and trade,

00:09:05.328 --> 00:09:09.823
that is ultimately reshaping it's climate.

00:09:11.075 --> 00:09:14.320
Really most of the energy

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:16.860
of this is coming from the global North.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.870
So where as development,

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.290
asks us to look at the problem of poverty

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:25.170
by looking closely at Southern countries.

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:30.170
I really think as many activists
in the South have long said

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:32.860
that the problem is in the North.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:36.400
This is where the majority of
consumption and greenhouse gas

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:38.360
emission is coming from.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:41.965
We are the people who set
the terms of global trade.

00:09:41.965 --> 00:09:45.910
You know, if we were to
just close the tax havens

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:48.900
and re-localize wealth back to the South

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:51.980
where it is being generated
and extracted from,

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:54.457
already we would be doing something

00:09:54.457 --> 00:09:57.050
that enables poverty reduction

00:09:57.050 --> 00:10:02.050
without it being premised,
necessarily on a growth model.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:05.050
So there are a number of
examples we could give

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:08.137
but I think development
unfortunately is constitutive

00:10:08.137 --> 00:10:09.450
of the problem.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:13.330
It's not something that's going
to be able to be moderated

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.100
by small technical fixes

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:17.263
or slight recalibration.

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:19.373
- Powerful points.

00:10:19.373 --> 00:10:22.300
So when we talk about how
do we redefine development,

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:24.594
these aren't small changes,
this is an entire transformation

00:10:24.594 --> 00:10:27.116
of the system that's needed.

00:10:27.116 --> 00:10:29.390
So I wanna go to Rory now

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:31.336
and Julie we'll come back to you.

00:10:31.336 --> 00:10:34.480
But Rory I know some of these dynamics

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:38.080
came into play when you were leading DFID

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:40.046
when you tried to make the case

00:10:40.046 --> 00:10:42.730
for environmental conservation work,

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:44.510
for climate change action,

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:47.180
it was perceived as in conflict

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:48.470
with some of the development work.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:51.280
So can you take us through
the challenges you faced

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:54.860
and how you ultimately
pushed through some of those?

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:57.210
- Well, thank you Catherine very much.

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:02.080
I think the, first thing is I came

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:04.570
to become a Secretary
of State, to run DFID

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:07.120
at a moment where the DFID budget was big.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:09.230
It was about 0.7% of UK GDP.

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:13.370
So spending about 20
billion US dollars a year,

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:17.463
and approximately half of that
was being spent in Africa.

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:22.500
And it was a time when there
was an increasing consensus,

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:26.113
that we should try to tackle,

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:31.310
an environmental and climate
crisis alongside poverty.

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:33.160
So there were World Bank reports

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:35.840
and OECD reports suggesting
that if we didn't manage

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:38.460
to deal with problems
with climate environment,

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:40.240
we would end up with hundreds of millions

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:41.810
more people in poverty.

00:11:43.536 --> 00:11:48.230
But when I actually began to try to shift,

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:50.770
the organization to focus
more on these issues,

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:54.040
I discovered there was an
enormous amount of resistance.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:58.210
And some of that of course,
Sunil has demonstrated,

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:00.200
has a very long history.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:01.720
So clearly what I was encountering

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:04.210
with civil servants were
memories of these debates

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:06.903
which had been going on
for many, many decades.

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:12.110
Part of it was as Sunil has
pointed to an instinctive

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:17.110
suspicion of projects
to do with non humans.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:20.650
It's a very important thing
that generally speaking

00:12:20.650 --> 00:12:23.720
when we talk about development,
we're talking about humans.

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:25.811
And people find it uncomfortable

00:12:25.811 --> 00:12:29.530
being (audio glitches) talk about animals

00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:31.953
or even about plants and habitats.

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:34.030
I think that's fascinating.

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:35.890
And that also goes simply

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:37.060
to the way that we measure things.

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:38.590
All the targets and different,

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:41.010
all the metrics were wrenched a round,

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:43.300
a hundred million girls
getting educational,

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.900
x hundred million people
being taken out of poverty.

00:12:47.390 --> 00:12:52.390
The second thing was the way,
the economist measured things.

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:55.890
And a very strong sense,

00:12:55.890 --> 00:12:58.720
from the development economists in DFID,

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:03.720
that by allocating money
to climate efficiently.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:06.760
And this was not the most effective way

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:08.310
of maximizing the number of people

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:11.246
we were going, (audio glitches)

00:13:11.246 --> 00:13:13.160
to take out of poverty.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:15.360
Now, there are many questions
around this kinds of models.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.330
Could we really take people out of poverty

00:13:17.330 --> 00:13:19.002
in the mechanistic way that they imagined?

00:13:19.002 --> 00:13:20.170
How did these models work?

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:23.002
Nevertheless, that was the way
in which they saw the world.

00:13:23.002 --> 00:13:25.067
And this was sort of
suspicions that people had.

00:13:25.067 --> 00:13:26.500
And I think the final thing,

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:28.630
and then I'll hand back to people.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:31.240
It's important to
understand that institutions

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:32.837
developed program (audio glitches),

00:13:33.869 --> 00:13:36.860
over 20, 30 years, they invest
in certain kinds of things

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:39.220
which are very difficult
for them to get out of.

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:41.480
So the Commonwealth
Development Corporation

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:43.590
would end up owning

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:46.833
a third of the electricity
generation of Tanzania.

00:13:46.833 --> 00:13:49.880
And that would be carbon-based
electricity generation

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:51.460
difficult to extract from.

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:53.350
We just given another $4 billion

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:55.170
that come with development corporation

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.521
and it wanted to make
more lending on energy.

00:13:57.521 --> 00:13:59.950
And most of the clients that it found

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:02.193
were carbon based energy crisis.

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:06.870
We were giving money
through development banks.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:09.310
World Bank, African Development Bank,

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:11.650
which were funding projects

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:14.660
with heavy oil generation in Senegal.

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:15.670
And it was extremely

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:18.360
if I said, " No, we're stopping all this."

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:19.590
The general response for civil servants

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:22.230
was could we avoid stopping,

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:23.340
could we have a glide path?

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:26.250
Could we just sign off
on this heavy oil plant?

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:28.430
And maybe in three or four years time,

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:29.420
we can move out of this.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:31.910
We need a longterm strategic position.

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:33.080
Some of that was genuine.

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:34.890
Some of that of course there's a hope

00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:37.630
that the secretary of
state will be reshuffled,

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:40.270
before they have to actually shift the way

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:42.743
in which the program balance works.

00:14:43.629 --> 00:14:44.840
I tend to supplement,

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:46.490
I mean there are many, many rich themes

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:47.490
that we can return to here,

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:50.763
but I think it's just, it was
a surprise to me to understand

00:14:50.763 --> 00:14:53.670
that something that
seemed, so straightforward

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:55.670
and oddly was very popular
with the British public.

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:58.890
It was no real resistance,
from the British public

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:00.830
that international development money

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:02.300
could be used for climate environments

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:03.730
in fact it was one of the easiest ways

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:05.163
to defending the 0.7%.

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:08.260
Is to say to people
this is our contribution

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:11.500
to the fight on global climate change.

00:15:11.500 --> 00:15:14.680
But the resistance from
within the organization,

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:16.030
was surprisingly strong.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:16.863
Enough for me.

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:18.673
- Thank you Rory.

00:15:18.673 --> 00:15:19.720
And you're right.

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:21.700
It's something that seems
so straight forward,

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:25.170
and yet actually implementing policies

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:28.370
that acknowledge the complexity
we're discussing today

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:30.510
is easier said than done.

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:32.598
And so that brings me to Rohini.

00:15:32.598 --> 00:15:34.954
Rohini I'm curious whether
you can help us connect

00:15:34.954 --> 00:15:38.260
this conversation, not only the topic

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:39.860
but the urgency of the topic.

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:41.990
To some of the conversations
happening right now

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:44.010
in development economics.

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:45.170
- Yeah, thanks Catherine.

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:48.493
And I'm very happy to be here.

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:51.900
I think, let me start by saying

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:53.640
what I'm not going to talk about,

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:57.920
which is, I think a very
common response right now

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:01.080
to think about this issue
is to invoke the idea

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:03.310
of de-growth.

00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:07.180
Is to say that, we've had
enough of economic growth

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:10.320
we need to move towards
a world where we focus

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.290
on not growing more.

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:16.000
And I think what Rory said
really brings up the side

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:18.870
of that no matter what your objective is,

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:22.930
they choose, do you have the
policy levers to get there.

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:24.790
So I think just moving a conversation

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:28.900
from saying, growth is bad for the climate

00:16:28.900 --> 00:16:30.520
let's move to de-growth,

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:32.260
is not going to be very helpful

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:36.740
because we don't actually
know how to do de-growth

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:39.870
any better necessarily than
how we know how to do growth

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:41.270
in the poorest parts of the world

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:42.913
that probably still need it.

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:44.910
So when we come to them

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:47.840
the issue of thinking about
how do we actually think

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:50.830
of policies for climate change,

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:53.350
I do think the issue
that comes up a lot of it

00:16:53.350 --> 00:16:55.420
has to do with political economy.

00:16:55.420 --> 00:16:57.570
And that both has to do with

00:16:57.570 --> 00:17:00.660
if you think about who's
affected by climate change,

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:03.320
but also I think the
difference in doing policies

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:06.940
for mitigation versus
those for adaptation.

00:17:06.940 --> 00:17:10.630
So going back to what
Rory was talking about

00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:14.188
development agencies,
remember having a fast

00:17:14.188 --> 00:17:15.930
(audio glitches) connection with someone

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:20.930
at one of the new large
investment banks in China,

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:23.300
who was describing that
they wanted to lend

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:26.040
for environmental purposes.

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:29.410
They wanted to lend
for ways of mitigation,

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:32.350
in a city, but it was very hard

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:34.760
to get all the stakeholders to agree.

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:37.270
So if you wanted to change for instance

00:17:37.270 --> 00:17:41.600
how urban energy system worked,

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:46.170
you have to go and get five
different constituents on.

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:48.990
What was much easier
to do was after crisis,

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:52.290
have to go and say let's build a seawall,

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:54.040
or to go and say after say,

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:56.730
as soon as I said the
ultra con crisis happens

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:59.270
to go out and say we need to provide those

00:17:59.270 --> 00:18:02.670
who've been rendered homeless, new homes.

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:06.690
And I think that's one
of the issues we face

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:09.052
is really if you want to address,

00:18:09.052 --> 00:18:11.300
(audio glitches) climate carefully

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:16.300
and alongside therefore
affect economic development,

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:19.720
we need to be thinking
a lot about mitigation.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:21.290
But it's hard to do

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:24.410
because the people who
are affected the most,

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:28.270
ex-ante they are also the
ones with the least bar.

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:30.620
And so in a society
with a lot of inequality

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:31.810
it's gonna be very hard

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:35.020
to persuade those who
are doing well today,

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:36.720
to look out for not themselves

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:39.060
but those were going to be
possibly rendered homeless

00:18:39.060 --> 00:18:40.623
by climate change tomorrow.

00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:44.800
So let me keep that as my
starting comments here.

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:45.720
That's right.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:46.755
- Thank you, appreciate it.

00:18:46.755 --> 00:18:48.600
And I do wanna go ahead
and welcome questions

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:49.490
from our audience.

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:51.640
I know we have a very engaged audience

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:53.330
and I'm always excited
to read your question.

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:55.510
So I have a few questions for the group,

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:57.560
but if you're joining us on Zoom,

00:18:57.560 --> 00:18:59.910
please go ahead and submit
any questions you have

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:00.947
in the Q&amp;A box.

00:19:00.947 --> 00:19:04.170
But I wanna Zoom in on something
Rohini was just mentioning

00:19:04.170 --> 00:19:05.760
in terms of inequality.

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:10.760
And, in line with our goal to
connect history and practice,

00:19:11.316 --> 00:19:13.380
I'd actually love to start with you Sunil

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:14.517
and then hear from others.

00:19:14.517 --> 00:19:18.310
What does history teach us
about the unequal impacts

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:20.060
of environmental degradation,

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:22.340
and how could this lens of inequality

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:25.700
help inform our conversation
around poverty alleviation

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:28.600
and climate change mitigation
and finding those synergies?

00:19:29.855 --> 00:19:32.010
- I think the lens of
inequality may well be the key

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:33.530
to sort of taking this conversation

00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:34.660
in a different direction.

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:37.580
Because I think we have
plenty of historical evidence

00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:39.920
on any time scale you
want to think through

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:43.240
whether it's several hundred
years or the last 50 years,

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:46.390
suggesting that in fact
environmental threats,

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:49.720
environmental risks for
most heavily on the poorest

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:52.407
and most marginalized people.

00:19:52.407 --> 00:19:54.770
They haven't been recognized as such

00:19:54.770 --> 00:19:56.260
perhaps until recently.

00:19:56.260 --> 00:19:59.910
And I mean I think that's one
of the historical trajectories

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:01.910
that I found interesting.

00:20:01.910 --> 00:20:04.480
The fact that this was
not necessarily visible

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:07.230
in the heyday of, state
directed development

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.939
of 1960s, 1970s.

00:20:09.939 --> 00:20:12.180
And yet I think that that is a place

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:14.876
where we can have a slightly
different kind of conversation,

00:20:14.876 --> 00:20:19.250
not so much about whether we
are for growth or de-growth,

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:23.810
but how, addressing inequality
is just a constitutive part

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:27.380
as Julie was saying, of
the problem writ large

00:20:27.380 --> 00:20:28.980
including the problem of climate change

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:30.810
but not limited to it.

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:33.059
I just also want to pick
up on what Julie was saying

00:20:33.059 --> 00:20:35.282
about this is not just about carbon.

00:20:35.282 --> 00:20:37.020
I think we do need to make sure

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:39.524
that we're having this conversation
in a way that recognizes

00:20:39.524 --> 00:20:43.120
that our different
scales in different kinds

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:44.480
of environmental crisis

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:48.913
are supercharging existing
patterns of inequality.

00:20:50.451 --> 00:20:52.830
- And on that point that
it's not just about carbon.

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:56.130
Julie I know your background
includes public health

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:58.600
and I wonder if you can speak a little bit

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:01.220
to when we talk about the
unequal impacts of climate change

00:21:01.220 --> 00:21:04.169
this is not just in the
future, this is very much now.

00:21:04.169 --> 00:21:06.699
So can you tell us a little bit more about

00:21:06.699 --> 00:21:10.020
I know sometimes you described
this in terms of the upstream

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:10.900
and the downstream

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:14.310
and where development tends to be geared.

00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:17.015
So one what do you think about inequality

00:21:17.015 --> 00:21:18.760
as maybe a useful lens

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:20.520
or useful way to reframe the conversation.

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:24.240
And two, from your background
not just your research

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:25.640
but also your work in public health,

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:28.500
how do you connect that
with this conversation?

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:32.774
- Sure. I think that downstream,

00:21:32.774 --> 00:21:37.220
there are, pressing an immediate concerns

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:41.830
like those that Rohini has been
gesturing toward absolutely.

00:21:41.830 --> 00:21:43.990
You know when people need
to eat, they need to eat.

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:45.754
When their homes are flooded,

00:21:45.754 --> 00:21:48.460
they need remediation.

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:51.780
But upstream, if we don't turn off the tap

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:54.006
that is creating this problem,

00:21:54.006 --> 00:21:59.006
inequality grows and the need
for remediation just grows.

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:03.085
And that is becoming
evident in our day to day.

00:22:03.085 --> 00:22:06.900
So when you think
upstream in public health

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:11.503
you realize that for example
in Africa where I work,

00:22:11.503 --> 00:22:14.800
what until recently was
a predominantly rural

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:16.800
and agricultural continent

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:20.370
has been urbanizing rapidly, rapidly.

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:24.470
There are people who are getting
on tiny unseaworthy boats

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:27.700
in order to try to reach a place

00:22:27.700 --> 00:22:30.000
where they will be able
to feed their families.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:33.070
And way upstream at the back of that,

00:22:33.070 --> 00:22:36.520
are a whole complicated
welter of relationships

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:39.970
and one of them are changes in land use

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:43.430
and land access patterns,
and the ongoing enclosure

00:22:43.430 --> 00:22:46.480
of the commons that is pushing
people off of their land

00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:48.630
and also the effects of climate change

00:22:48.630 --> 00:22:51.750
that are making it so they
can no longer make a go of it

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:55.777
as a fishermen or as farmers, et cetera.

00:22:55.777 --> 00:22:58.070
If we think in terms of the pandemic,

00:22:58.070 --> 00:23:01.790
we know that the
evisceration of wild lands

00:23:01.790 --> 00:23:06.790
is hemming in, wild species in ways

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:10.160
that are facilitating zoonosis
we've seen that with Ebola

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:13.630
now we see it again, with COVID.

00:23:13.630 --> 00:23:18.630
So I do think of course that
mitigation at the coalface

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:20.830
needs to happen.

00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:23.670
But I am a de-growth proponent

00:23:23.670 --> 00:23:26.180
I don't see any other way.

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:29.250
I don't think we can keep
creating the problem,

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:31.520
and then trying to mitigate it.

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:34.620
There are people who fall
through the cracks continually

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:35.543
when that happens.

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:38.710
- I wanted to ask Rory a question.

00:23:38.710 --> 00:23:40.530
Because Rory we heard
some of the challenges

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:42.378
you faced within DFID.

00:23:42.378 --> 00:23:45.853
And I wonder as Julie just mentioned,

00:23:46.870 --> 00:23:51.600
public awareness of some of
the very immediate problems

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:55.520
with the damage humans
are doing to the planet

00:23:55.520 --> 00:23:56.750
public awareness is growing.

00:23:56.750 --> 00:24:00.470
I mean COVID-19 and zoonotic
disease is one clear example

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:02.610
of how environmental destruction

00:24:02.610 --> 00:24:04.640
is having very immediate human impacts.

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:06.070
Do you think it would be different

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:07.070
if you were leading,

00:24:08.792 --> 00:24:10.240
DFID now as CDO,

00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:15.240
if you were leading the UK's
aid portfolio right now,

00:24:15.860 --> 00:24:17.040
could you make a different argument?

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:18.210
Could you build on that momentum

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:21.007
and can other leaders do
the same at this moment?

00:24:21.007 --> 00:24:22.976
- I think you can.

00:24:22.976 --> 00:24:25.523
But it's worth bearing in mind that,

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:30.200
it's still unfortunately
true that most polling

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:31.693
in most developed countries,

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:35.150
puts environments surprisingly low down

00:24:35.150 --> 00:24:36.220
on versus priorities.

00:24:36.220 --> 00:24:37.930
And the amount that they're
prepared to spend on it

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:39.510
is still very limited.

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:42.030
I discovered this for example
running to be mayor of London

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:44.671
when I was trying to emphasize

00:24:44.671 --> 00:24:46.560
even which you would have thought

00:24:46.560 --> 00:24:48.450
would really matter to
people like air quality,

00:24:48.450 --> 00:24:51.120
let alone, things like planting trees.

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:53.863
I mean it turned out consistently,

00:24:55.190 --> 00:24:56.760
that the campaign managers,

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:57.740
the putting advisors

00:24:57.740 --> 00:25:00.050
would say Rory just
stop talking about that

00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:02.250
people wanna talk about knife crime.

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:04.560
And they would show data,

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:05.670
credible amount of data

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:07.950
to show that people were not prepared

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:09.670
to spend even,

00:25:09.670 --> 00:25:11.743
50 pounds a month,

00:25:12.650 --> 00:25:14.280
to try to contribute
towards these associates.

00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:16.750
So I think that's an important background.

00:25:16.750 --> 00:25:19.180
On the specific question of
whether you can shift people

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:20.580
then develop budgets, yes you can.

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:24.920
But remember, that's
partly because in the UK

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:27.433
so long as we have the 0.7% target,

00:25:28.310 --> 00:25:31.000
the voters subconsciously felt

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:33.370
okay that money is
already going to be spent

00:25:33.370 --> 00:25:34.590
on this kind of stuff anyway.

00:25:34.590 --> 00:25:36.350
So the question I'm not being asked

00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:39.670
is not would I rather that
money was spent on healthcare

00:25:39.670 --> 00:25:42.350
in Britain or right in Britain
or a school in Britain.

00:25:42.350 --> 00:25:45.150
This money has got to be
spent abroad and therefore,

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:46.660
would I be happy,

00:25:46.660 --> 00:25:48.630
that it was spent on
environment and climate work

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:50.660
or would I rather it was
spent on some other thing.

00:25:50.660 --> 00:25:52.470
And framed like that,

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:54.090
it was very easy to make the argument

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:57.480
for farming to climate
work, with the public.

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:00.040
The problem though, just
to sort of cycle back

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:00.873
to some of the issue

00:26:00.873 --> 00:26:02.960
that different people
have been talking about.

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:06.460
I think there's the big
problem which Julie has raised.

00:26:06.460 --> 00:26:08.970
Which is that it's almost impossible

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:10.890
to get a political constituency

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:14.133
behind questioning growth itself,

00:26:15.063 --> 00:26:16.450
in most developed countries.

00:26:16.450 --> 00:26:18.203
Is very, very, very difficult.

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:20.940
Even the Green Party in Britain struggles

00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:22.833
to actually make those kinds of arguments.

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:25.070
But I think the other thing

00:26:25.070 --> 00:26:27.250
is that there is a real problem,

00:26:27.250 --> 00:26:29.710
evaluating the quality
of environmental projects

00:26:29.710 --> 00:26:32.310
on the ground and what their
impact is likely to be.

00:26:33.430 --> 00:26:36.310
So for example if you were
doing flood management now,

00:26:36.310 --> 00:26:40.050
it would be very tempting to
talk about natural solutions

00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:41.100
to flood management.

00:26:41.100 --> 00:26:44.499
So planting trees for example
to stop water movement.

00:26:44.499 --> 00:26:49.499
But explaining clearly
that actually our modeling

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:51.000
on this is extremely complicated

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:52.100
that it matters a lot

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:53.640
if you're trying to
block flooding with trees

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:55.890
what season it is, whether
there leaves on the trees,

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:57.730
how old the trees are,

00:26:57.730 --> 00:27:00.510
that actually that type
of management works better

00:27:00.510 --> 00:27:01.970
when the trees are 20 years old

00:27:01.970 --> 00:27:04.360
when they just being planted
and what all that means

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:07.620
for somebody downstream, who
might be flooded next year

00:27:07.620 --> 00:27:09.100
and where the choice is being made

00:27:09.100 --> 00:27:10.420
not to build a flood wall

00:27:10.420 --> 00:27:11.730
but instead to plant some trees

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:13.330
that are gonna have some
impacts in 20 years time

00:27:13.330 --> 00:27:14.780
there is a difficult

00:27:14.780 --> 00:27:16.590
I think it's also true
when we make arguments

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:17.730
on organic farming.

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:19.530
Again many of them have a great enthusiasm

00:27:19.530 --> 00:27:23.360
for organic farming but when
it actually comes to explaining

00:27:23.360 --> 00:27:25.540
to a farmer in Zambia

00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:28.260
what that's really gonna
mean in terms of the yield

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:29.920
from a hectare of crops,

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:32.210
things become much more complicated.

00:27:32.210 --> 00:27:36.150
We don't really have good natural
capital measures in place.

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:37.243
And the final thing I wanted to say

00:27:37.243 --> 00:27:39.510
is that again you know
this is quite granular

00:27:39.510 --> 00:27:44.010
but it is very striking
how incredibly expensive,

00:27:44.010 --> 00:27:47.733
a loss of current proposals
on habitat restoration.

00:27:49.050 --> 00:27:51.070
I was the environment minister in Britain

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:53.860
before I was the international
development minister.

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:57.480
We were doing peatland
restoration projects.

00:27:57.480 --> 00:28:00.560
Restoring peatland where
we would be spending

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:03.950
40 million pounds on reseeding,

00:28:03.950 --> 00:28:06.630
a couple of thousand hectares of peatland

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:09.593
by dropping sphagnum
moss from helicopters.

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:12.910
They've just announced another
400 million pound project

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:14.380
to this sort of Britain.

00:28:14.380 --> 00:28:16.000
And you can absolutely understand

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:17.870
I'm not gonna defend my defeat economists

00:28:17.870 --> 00:28:21.550
why they might stick their
hands up and say what really,

00:28:21.550 --> 00:28:22.720
is that the very best thing

00:28:22.720 --> 00:28:24.070
that you can think to do with your money?

00:28:24.070 --> 00:28:28.390
Can you really, demonstrate
the consequence of that?

00:28:28.390 --> 00:28:30.520
So I suppose what I'm talking
about is bridging the gap

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:32.910
between similar absolutely correct point

00:28:32.910 --> 00:28:35.980
that as a general statement,

00:28:35.980 --> 00:28:37.620
the most marginalized poorest communities

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:39.707
suffer most from (audio glitches).

00:28:41.260 --> 00:28:42.320
That needs to be linked

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:43.680
to the very specific question

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:46.130
of are you prepared to
spend 400 million pounds

00:28:46.130 --> 00:28:47.390
on peatland restoration

00:28:47.390 --> 00:28:49.830
by dropping sphagnum
moss from helicopters?

00:28:49.830 --> 00:28:51.520
Or is it something else that
you wanna do with your money?

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:55.233
And we're surprisingly bad
at answering those questions.

00:28:57.270 --> 00:28:59.470
- I wanna bring in some
questions from the audience here

00:28:59.470 --> 00:29:02.600
and one of them has to do with growth.

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:07.100
And I feel like we might
have some differing opinions

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:08.830
among our panelists on
the way forward here

00:29:08.830 --> 00:29:11.100
which I think it's always
interesting to engage

00:29:11.100 --> 00:29:12.532
in some productive debate.

00:29:12.532 --> 00:29:15.470
This question is posed to Julie,
but I'd love to hear Rohini

00:29:15.470 --> 00:29:16.303
on this as well

00:29:16.303 --> 00:29:17.783
and anyone else who wants to jump in.

00:29:17.783 --> 00:29:20.920
But Sanjeev Kumar asks Julie,

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:23.130
what would it take to
convince the skeptics

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:25.530
of the de-growth idea
to change their minds?

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:28.000
Rory just noted that
that's very hard to do,

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:31.610
and I know you believe
that's the only way forward.

00:29:31.610 --> 00:29:35.053
So what does it take to bring
others on board with you?

00:29:36.042 --> 00:29:39.263
- Thanks and thanks for that question.

00:29:40.140 --> 00:29:42.920
I actually think that
the public is onboard

00:29:42.920 --> 00:29:45.330
with a number of de-growth
strategies already

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:47.300
like the basic income grant.

00:29:47.300 --> 00:29:49.190
Something that has turned out

00:29:49.190 --> 00:29:50.270
to be quite popular

00:29:50.270 --> 00:29:53.350
here in the United States of all places,

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:57.260
which is definitely a de-growth strategy

00:29:57.260 --> 00:29:58.890
and something that in the US

00:29:58.890 --> 00:30:00.550
we were always told you can't have

00:30:00.550 --> 00:30:03.580
and then after the pandemic,
it turned out suddenly

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:06.270
for a few months there, we
had a basic income grant.

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:08.478
I think the closing of tax havens,

00:30:08.478 --> 00:30:13.478
is quite popular among the non Davos set.

00:30:14.020 --> 00:30:18.030
Most of us are deeply
offended that some people pay

00:30:18.030 --> 00:30:20.330
and many people really don't

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:21.940
particularly those with a lot of money

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:25.070
or corporations that
have places to hide it.

00:30:25.070 --> 00:30:30.060
I think, having standards
against producing goods

00:30:30.060 --> 00:30:33.670
that are meant to become
obsolete and disposable

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:35.240
within a year or two,

00:30:35.240 --> 00:30:40.100
and be replaced, would be quite
popular among many people.

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:44.040
So I think that there are a
number of de-growth strategies

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:48.150
out there that are already popular,

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:50.120
and then you can look around the world

00:30:50.120 --> 00:30:51.300
at different countries

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:55.960
that are taking on de-growth platforms

00:30:55.960 --> 00:30:59.970
as part of their policymaking.

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:04.160
Whether it's the government
of Bolivia or Ecuador,

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:05.630
or whether it's New Zealand

00:31:05.630 --> 00:31:10.630
which has unhitched it's
the drivers of it's policy,

00:31:11.370 --> 00:31:13.863
from a growth metric.

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:18.150
So I think it's a debate and it's a debate

00:31:18.150 --> 00:31:20.140
that many people are ready to have,

00:31:20.140 --> 00:31:25.140
and the more that we unpack it
and start talking it through

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:28.523
and realize that de-growth
is not the same thing

00:31:28.523 --> 00:31:31.630
it's just letting the economy crash out,

00:31:31.630 --> 00:31:36.630
that in fact it's a very
planned and step wise move

00:31:36.970 --> 00:31:41.050
into economic systems that are more fair,

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:44.520
more just, and that it will
not lead us off of precipice.

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:47.090
I think people are rational
and they're empirical

00:31:47.090 --> 00:31:48.723
and they will get behind it.

00:31:50.210 --> 00:31:51.140
- What do you think Rohini?

00:31:51.140 --> 00:31:54.090
I know you've began your conversation,

00:31:54.090 --> 00:31:55.010
mentioning de-growth

00:31:55.010 --> 00:31:56.690
and actually that you
wouldn't be advocating for it

00:31:56.690 --> 00:31:59.670
but as we unpack it,
how would you respond?

00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:03.410
- Yeah. So I think it's
useful to distinguish

00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:06.000
between de-growth within an economy

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:07.557
or within a local area.

00:32:07.557 --> 00:32:11.430
And the question of what it means

00:32:11.430 --> 00:32:13.600
in terms of global redistribution.

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:14.973
And I think this would go back

00:32:14.973 --> 00:32:17.600
to a lot of the debates
that we've been seeing

00:32:17.600 --> 00:32:19.610
in the aid world and development

00:32:19.610 --> 00:32:23.710
is that as even (audio glitches)
are becoming more unequal,

00:32:23.710 --> 00:32:25.173
it's harder very often,

00:32:25.173 --> 00:32:30.173
to have a case both of
saying, let's not just have

00:32:30.830 --> 00:32:34.430
say de-growth within the
US or in New Zealand,

00:32:34.430 --> 00:32:38.160
but let's also send money
and resources to Botswana

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:40.050
or to other parts the world.

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:42.760
I think the combination
of the two is hard,

00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:44.640
but if you really want
to affect climate change

00:32:44.640 --> 00:32:47.790
we have to be thinking
about the global stage.

00:32:47.790 --> 00:32:50.520
I mean I think that's one
of the big (audio glitches)

00:32:50.520 --> 00:32:55.040
difference within say COVID
where people have recognized

00:32:55.040 --> 00:32:56.500
that their health depends on others

00:32:56.500 --> 00:32:59.160
but they still see it as
a very domestic issue.

00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:00.320
I think everything for instance

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:02.460
we are seeing right now
on vaccine nationalism

00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:06.570
is very much I want my
neighborhood vaccinated,

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:08.630
but it doesn't really ring true

00:33:08.630 --> 00:33:11.160
for people to think that,
vaccination in Canada

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:13.500
is necessary for me to be protected.

00:33:13.500 --> 00:33:16.670
So I think similarly, when
we talk about de-growth

00:33:16.670 --> 00:33:19.400
and this is a thing
where Sunil started from,

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:22.170
you're going to get very
little constituents,

00:33:22.170 --> 00:33:24.780
in favor of de-growth in say Sierra Leone

00:33:24.780 --> 00:33:26.540
or in the poorest parts of India

00:33:26.540 --> 00:33:29.580
where you still believe that
a lot of very poor people.

00:33:29.580 --> 00:33:32.128
So if you have to take
de-growth seriously,

00:33:32.128 --> 00:33:34.580
at a level that's going
to affect climate change

00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:38.220
we have to ask how are
we going to accompany,

00:33:38.220 --> 00:33:41.220
single individuals, or single cities,

00:33:41.220 --> 00:33:45.650
and the rich not being
happy to (audio glitches)

00:33:45.650 --> 00:33:47.790
quickly recognizing the
type of redistribution

00:33:47.790 --> 00:33:49.340
that's going to be needed.

00:33:49.340 --> 00:33:52.620
And I think that requires
a global coalition.

00:33:52.620 --> 00:33:54.580
And that's why I think,

00:33:54.580 --> 00:33:57.630
my push has been a lot more
let's talk about the policies

00:33:57.630 --> 00:34:00.810
we need, to achieve those outcomes.

00:34:00.810 --> 00:34:03.240
And maybe de-growth will be
part of it for the rich economy

00:34:03.240 --> 00:34:06.263
but it's certainly not going
to be enough on it's own.

00:34:07.640 --> 00:34:09.430
- Here is one more
question related to growth.

00:34:09.430 --> 00:34:10.550
I see Rory wanted to come in.

00:34:10.550 --> 00:34:14.001
So Rory maybe you can respond
to this question as well.

00:34:14.001 --> 00:34:17.670
Jacob Rink, asking about this growth,

00:34:17.670 --> 00:34:19.240
de-growth conversation.

00:34:19.240 --> 00:34:21.030
Asked when we speak
about growth or de-growth

00:34:21.030 --> 00:34:23.480
does it make sense to
differentiate between resource

00:34:23.480 --> 00:34:24.660
and carbon intensive growth

00:34:24.660 --> 00:34:27.350
and growth in services such
as education and health?

00:34:27.350 --> 00:34:28.940
And I think,

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:30.780
we would agree to that
and I see nodding heads.

00:34:30.780 --> 00:34:31.613
- Yes we are.

00:34:31.613 --> 00:34:32.760
And I think it makes a lot of sense

00:34:32.760 --> 00:34:33.870
to make all these distinctions.

00:34:33.870 --> 00:34:36.660
I mean this is a very, very
difficult thing to resolve

00:34:36.660 --> 00:34:41.293
this question of, how much
willingness there is behind this.

00:34:42.440 --> 00:34:44.170
And Julie may well be vindicated.

00:34:44.170 --> 00:34:46.470
I mean it may well be that
in 40 or 50 years time,

00:34:46.470 --> 00:34:48.640
she'll turn out to be right.

00:34:48.640 --> 00:34:50.820
It's very difficult to prove.

00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:54.973
Generally people who are
campaigning for this kind of thing,

00:34:55.850 --> 00:34:58.650
will tend to be on the optimistic side

00:34:58.650 --> 00:35:00.450
and generally working politicians like me

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:03.100
will tend to be on the somewhat
cynical pessimistic side

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:06.550
about the ability to inspire populations

00:35:06.550 --> 00:35:08.924
to do things that hit their pocketbooks.

00:35:08.924 --> 00:35:12.730
And it's so one of the interesting things

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:14.080
about politics and changes,

00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:15.280
these things are so unpredictable

00:35:15.280 --> 00:35:18.380
and we clearly can feel this
in relation to civil rights,

00:35:18.380 --> 00:35:21.170
we can feel this in relation
to many other changes

00:35:21.170 --> 00:35:22.410
that we've seen in the 20th century.

00:35:22.410 --> 00:35:25.433
So it may be that, this will shift.

00:35:27.781 --> 00:35:30.460
I just say that it would be difficult

00:35:30.460 --> 00:35:35.460
to find many working politicians
and major political parties

00:35:35.750 --> 00:35:37.100
in Europe or the United States

00:35:37.100 --> 00:35:41.070
that would really imagine
selling something as ambitious

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:41.990
as Julie is speaking about.

00:35:41.990 --> 00:35:44.503
That's not to say that it won't happen,

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:48.410
but it's gonna be a hell of a fight,

00:35:48.410 --> 00:35:50.843
if we're really gonna get
anywhere close to that.

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:56.020
We're talking about much,
much more incremental.

00:35:56.020 --> 00:35:57.300
- Sorry about that Rory.

00:35:57.300 --> 00:35:59.920
Sunil did I see that you wanted
to jump in on this question?

00:35:59.920 --> 00:36:02.060
No. We have another question coming in

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:03.820
that I'd love to ask you all about.

00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:06.630
So one of the themes that
came up in our conversation

00:36:06.630 --> 00:36:11.630
is, who's responsible
to take action right?

00:36:12.290 --> 00:36:15.379
And that developed countries,

00:36:15.379 --> 00:36:18.750
it's often argued that the countries

00:36:18.750 --> 00:36:21.270
that got us into this climate mess,

00:36:21.270 --> 00:36:23.690
should be at the forefront
of getting us out of it.

00:36:23.690 --> 00:36:27.802
So when it comes to what that looks like,

00:36:27.802 --> 00:36:32.802
there is a question along the
lines of a, recent Biden...

00:36:33.250 --> 00:36:34.450
Here let me find this question.

00:36:34.450 --> 00:36:35.423
Where did it go?

00:36:36.922 --> 00:36:39.550
There is a question around
the Biden administration

00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:43.303
setting the social cost
of carbon to $51 a ton.

00:36:44.150 --> 00:36:47.010
And this attendee anonymous asks,

00:36:47.010 --> 00:36:48.610
this is still a significantly lower

00:36:48.610 --> 00:36:51.490
than many other social
cost of carbon values.

00:36:51.490 --> 00:36:54.230
What is the panel's view of the efficacy

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:55.330
of social cost of carbon

00:36:55.330 --> 00:36:57.730
and mitigating risks to the
populations being discussed?

00:36:57.730 --> 00:37:00.300
And maybe I'll broaden that question.

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:01.510
Any panelists who wanna comment

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:03.700
on the social cost of carbon specifically

00:37:03.700 --> 00:37:05.080
interested to hear your views,

00:37:05.080 --> 00:37:08.720
but more broadly I will
say, we should talk perhaps

00:37:08.720 --> 00:37:10.810
about the US as a leader here,

00:37:10.810 --> 00:37:12.440
we have a new administration,

00:37:12.440 --> 00:37:15.010
new policies being rolled out for climate.

00:37:15.010 --> 00:37:16.720
Might that be an opportunity

00:37:16.720 --> 00:37:18.240
to harness some of the synergies

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:19.900
we're discussing right now

00:37:19.900 --> 00:37:23.060
to change the conversation
around climate and development?

00:37:23.060 --> 00:37:24.960
So since this question was posed

00:37:24.960 --> 00:37:26.150
about the Biden administration

00:37:26.150 --> 00:37:28.400
would love to hear any
thoughts on that opportunity.

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:30.580
- Let me comment very quickly
and then head of to us.

00:37:30.580 --> 00:37:32.210
I mean I would've thought,

00:37:32.210 --> 00:37:35.860
one thing is just to remind the US,

00:37:35.860 --> 00:37:38.550
that there is an enormous
amount that they can do,

00:37:38.550 --> 00:37:41.400
just to catch up even with,

00:37:41.400 --> 00:37:43.840
offenders like the United Kingdom.

00:37:43.840 --> 00:37:46.850
I mean the UK has had
the paragon of virtue

00:37:46.850 --> 00:37:48.470
it's not New Zealand right?

00:37:48.470 --> 00:37:50.850
But it's managed to significantly

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:53.023
reduce et cetera trustee consumption.

00:37:54.580 --> 00:37:56.560
It's managed to move to a situation

00:37:56.560 --> 00:37:58.570
where 40% of our generation at the moment

00:37:58.570 --> 00:38:00.260
comes from renewables.

00:38:00.260 --> 00:38:01.810
And it's done it relatively quickly

00:38:01.810 --> 00:38:02.950
and relatively painlessly.

00:38:02.950 --> 00:38:05.690
And the US hasn't even begun
to stretch it's muscles

00:38:05.690 --> 00:38:10.547
to match the standard set
by major European countries.

00:38:11.991 --> 00:38:13.392
- Thank you Rory.
- So I think...

00:38:13.392 --> 00:38:18.392
Sorry, the reality is that the status quo

00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:20.993
is extremely expensive.

00:38:22.640 --> 00:38:24.761
A year ago like a 10th,

00:38:24.761 --> 00:38:29.761
I'm making up this figure
of Australia was on fire.

00:38:30.050 --> 00:38:33.130
Every year now, a huge part of California

00:38:33.130 --> 00:38:37.150
which is a major driver
of our economy is on fire.

00:38:37.150 --> 00:38:39.933
Look at what just happened in Texas.

00:38:40.820 --> 00:38:43.730
And before that would happen in Louisiana

00:38:43.730 --> 00:38:45.880
and what will happen
later in the next place,

00:38:45.880 --> 00:38:46.713
in the next place,

00:38:46.713 --> 00:38:51.713
this is just extremely
extraordinarily expensive.

00:38:51.837 --> 00:38:54.720
But that money doesn't
disappear from the economy.

00:38:54.720 --> 00:38:56.930
People make bank off of this.

00:38:56.930 --> 00:39:00.103
People are making bank
off of this pandemic.

00:39:00.103 --> 00:39:04.170
And if we don't start to
think about relationships

00:39:04.170 --> 00:39:08.750
as relationships, but
instead as siloed events,

00:39:08.750 --> 00:39:12.660
I think we are misapprehending

00:39:12.660 --> 00:39:14.403
some of the drivers,

00:39:16.000 --> 00:39:18.130
of our current predicament.

00:39:18.130 --> 00:39:22.433
Including and perhaps especially
here in the United States.

00:39:23.900 --> 00:39:24.850
- Thanks Julie and Rohini

00:39:24.850 --> 00:39:26.840
I know you want us to jump in as well.

00:39:26.840 --> 00:39:28.545
- Yeah, I just wanted to enter,

00:39:28.545 --> 00:39:30.910
as you Julie's comments
are usually useful way

00:39:30.910 --> 00:39:35.000
for me to jump in which is,
one way that economists respond

00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:37.250
to this and perhaps the
social cost of carbon

00:39:37.250 --> 00:39:38.510
epitomizes a test to believe

00:39:38.510 --> 00:39:40.410
that if we accept this as the cost

00:39:40.410 --> 00:39:43.240
we are going to accept a higher prices.

00:39:43.240 --> 00:39:46.390
And so for instance thinking
about carbon pricing

00:39:46.390 --> 00:39:51.390
are kind of cap and trade
systems, is very popular

00:39:52.640 --> 00:39:53.930
the economist very often for saying

00:39:53.930 --> 00:39:56.520
just let the markets enter and resolve it.

00:39:56.520 --> 00:39:58.210
And I think as Julie pointed out

00:39:58.210 --> 00:40:00.180
the Texas example is actually an example

00:40:00.180 --> 00:40:02.900
where you were trying to
get the markets to work well

00:40:02.900 --> 00:40:04.250
and they didn't.

00:40:04.250 --> 00:40:05.310
And it's good to remember

00:40:05.310 --> 00:40:10.310
why kind of even if you might
have a higher Instacart one

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:13.950
you don't want to rely just on markets.

00:40:13.950 --> 00:40:15.670
So I think a couple of reasons for this.

00:40:15.670 --> 00:40:20.080
So the first is pricing
is sometimes for consumers

00:40:20.080 --> 00:40:23.176
much more sensitive in
some sectors than others.

00:40:23.176 --> 00:40:26.437
So for instance gasoline,

00:40:26.437 --> 00:40:30.060
politicians don't want to
raise the prices on gasoline.

00:40:30.060 --> 00:40:32.190
So having kind of carbon taxes

00:40:32.190 --> 00:40:34.470
that are going to translate
into the gasoline sector

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:36.370
is going to be much harder

00:40:36.370 --> 00:40:39.320
than trying to introduce carbon pricing

00:40:39.320 --> 00:40:41.170
and maybe electricity or someplace

00:40:41.170 --> 00:40:43.570
but it's a much more if you want hidden.

00:40:43.570 --> 00:40:46.100
And so I think pushing
on just one instrument

00:40:46.100 --> 00:40:47.690
can often defeat the purpose

00:40:47.690 --> 00:40:49.930
because you may just
want to play off the fact

00:40:49.930 --> 00:40:51.640
that consumers care differently

00:40:51.640 --> 00:40:54.610
about the marginal cost
of different goods.

00:40:54.610 --> 00:40:55.620
And then the second thing

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:57.794
which I think comes from the Texas example

00:40:57.794 --> 00:41:00.970
is I think what we saw is that yes

00:41:00.970 --> 00:41:03.730
prices were able to adjust quite fast,

00:41:03.730 --> 00:41:08.730
but the investor need to make
for these one off possible

00:41:09.280 --> 00:41:11.040
frigid weather in Texas,

00:41:11.040 --> 00:41:12.860
maybe seen as so rare

00:41:12.860 --> 00:41:15.110
that the person who's building the pipe

00:41:15.110 --> 00:41:16.770
is just not going to invest in that

00:41:16.770 --> 00:41:18.260
because it's not worth their while.

00:41:18.260 --> 00:41:20.080
They're not getting a huge return

00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:23.320
when the energy price goes sky high.

00:41:23.320 --> 00:41:25.530
So the energy provider gets it

00:41:25.530 --> 00:41:27.930
but not the person who's
maintaining the pipes.

00:41:27.930 --> 00:41:30.073
And so again I think this comes
to the issue of the system

00:41:30.073 --> 00:41:34.080
that if you want to rely
on markets and pricing,

00:41:34.080 --> 00:41:36.120
we have to realize that
there are a lot of players

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:37.420
we have to take along.

00:41:37.420 --> 00:41:40.910
And that will often bring us
too much blunter regulation.

00:41:40.910 --> 00:41:42.230
And so I think it's good

00:41:42.230 --> 00:41:43.870
if you increase the social cost of carbon

00:41:43.870 --> 00:41:46.080
but I think the danger is to then think

00:41:46.080 --> 00:41:47.770
that the markets will take care of it.

00:41:47.770 --> 00:41:50.070
I think that just doesn't happen.

00:41:50.070 --> 00:41:51.870
- So we had a question
on social cost of carbon

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:52.703
which we addressed,

00:41:52.703 --> 00:41:55.240
but we've so many great
questions coming in.

00:41:55.240 --> 00:41:56.073
So keep them coming.

00:41:56.073 --> 00:41:58.330
I wanna group together three questions,

00:41:58.330 --> 00:42:00.630
that also relate to responses

00:42:00.630 --> 00:42:01.890
while we're talking about responses.

00:42:01.890 --> 00:42:04.000
And responses by different actors.

00:42:04.000 --> 00:42:05.570
So we have one question

00:42:05.570 --> 00:42:09.590
around the role of development
finance institutions.

00:42:09.590 --> 00:42:11.210
So the question is, any thoughts

00:42:11.210 --> 00:42:14.770
on current efforts by DFS and
international organizations

00:42:14.770 --> 00:42:18.780
on engaging with Ifs,
insurers and asset managers,

00:42:18.780 --> 00:42:22.280
to improve environment
related controls and policies,

00:42:22.280 --> 00:42:24.090
exiting coal for instance?

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:25.020
So that's one question.

00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:26.790
I'm gonna throw three
out and then we'll see

00:42:26.790 --> 00:42:30.740
how we can talk around
these questions as a panel.

00:42:30.740 --> 00:42:31.573
There is another question

00:42:31.573 --> 00:42:33.250
around the role of the private sector.

00:42:33.250 --> 00:42:35.150
How do you incentivize the private sector

00:42:35.150 --> 00:42:39.180
to create green economy jobs,
that also alleviate poverty?

00:42:39.180 --> 00:42:41.180
London stock exchange
has created a category

00:42:41.180 --> 00:42:42.880
called the green economy mark

00:42:42.880 --> 00:42:45.390
to attract issuers for example.

00:42:45.390 --> 00:42:47.680
And now we move into the policy realm.

00:42:47.680 --> 00:42:50.430
To what extent can environment
strategies be combined

00:42:50.430 --> 00:42:52.090
into development policies,

00:42:52.090 --> 00:42:54.810
to create new jobs that
would advance both goals

00:42:54.810 --> 00:42:55.643
at the same time?

00:42:55.643 --> 00:42:57.360
So related questions

00:42:57.360 --> 00:43:00.170
around the role of development
finance institutions,

00:43:00.170 --> 00:43:02.990
the private sector, and policies.

00:43:02.990 --> 00:43:05.150
Combining environment
and development policy

00:43:05.150 --> 00:43:06.510
something Rory touched on earlier

00:43:06.510 --> 00:43:08.280
but all of this has to do

00:43:08.280 --> 00:43:11.660
with how we respond and how
different sectors respond.

00:43:11.660 --> 00:43:13.330
So any thoughts on any of those questions

00:43:13.330 --> 00:43:16.190
I'll see which of our panelists
wants to jump in Rory.

00:43:16.190 --> 00:43:17.790
- Well, let me firstly say,

00:43:17.790 --> 00:43:22.110
that the overall theory
behind those questions

00:43:22.110 --> 00:43:23.100
has to be right.

00:43:23.100 --> 00:43:26.230
I mean, this far more money

00:43:26.230 --> 00:43:28.340
sitting around in these
great financial centers

00:43:28.340 --> 00:43:30.810
than there are in the development nations.

00:43:30.810 --> 00:43:33.050
And if there were some
intelligent mechanism

00:43:33.050 --> 00:43:35.150
for working out how you unlocked the money

00:43:35.150 --> 00:43:37.035
that was in New York
or the city of London,

00:43:37.035 --> 00:43:39.770
and directed it towards
the kind of projects

00:43:39.770 --> 00:43:43.600
we want to do on a farm
climate or poverty alleviation,

00:43:43.600 --> 00:43:45.797
that would be a very smart thing to do.

00:43:45.797 --> 00:43:48.130
My role seems to be in the state,

00:43:48.130 --> 00:43:49.890
than saying that in practice of course

00:43:49.890 --> 00:43:51.610
this turns out to be
unbelievably difficult.

00:43:51.610 --> 00:43:54.900
I mean, so I spent a lot of
time crushing around the city

00:43:54.900 --> 00:43:57.060
of London trying to work
with the insurance industry,

00:43:57.060 --> 00:44:00.540
trying to work with different
forms of investment firm,

00:44:00.540 --> 00:44:02.603
trying to define green investment funds

00:44:02.603 --> 00:44:04.870
and the amounts of money in the end

00:44:04.870 --> 00:44:06.710
that we actually got,

00:44:06.710 --> 00:44:09.383
available to be invested
was surprisingly limited.

00:44:10.430 --> 00:44:14.582
Because there seems to be
fundamentally a problem

00:44:14.582 --> 00:44:15.932
generally (audio glitches),

00:44:17.420 --> 00:44:21.260
which is that these things
are not as profitable

00:44:21.260 --> 00:44:22.573
as we want to pretend.

00:44:23.730 --> 00:44:25.430
There is a reason,

00:44:25.430 --> 00:44:30.430
why generally speaking
effectively public grant money,

00:44:31.693 --> 00:44:36.670
is more likely, to be able to
go into some of these sectors

00:44:36.670 --> 00:44:39.243
than credit capital.

00:44:39.243 --> 00:44:43.090
And it's a lesson we learned
in a hundred different ways.

00:44:43.090 --> 00:44:45.110
I mean, one of the most depressing things

00:44:45.110 --> 00:44:47.200
about development in Africa,

00:44:47.200 --> 00:44:49.560
is that it's very easy
to make a presentation

00:44:49.560 --> 00:44:51.740
about the extraordinary
potential of Africa

00:44:51.740 --> 00:44:54.120
the incredible human capital,

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:56.260
the incredible natural capital,

00:44:56.260 --> 00:44:58.643
a great story about
how this is the future.

00:44:59.562 --> 00:45:01.810
And you make those
presentations again and again

00:45:01.810 --> 00:45:03.650
while being aware that British Airways

00:45:03.650 --> 00:45:05.430
is shutting all the flights into Africa

00:45:05.430 --> 00:45:08.180
the major hedge funds are
drawing out of Africa.

00:45:08.180 --> 00:45:09.500
Barclay's Bank is getting rid

00:45:09.500 --> 00:45:11.050
of all it's branches in Africa.

00:45:12.910 --> 00:45:16.980
So there is a problem
that we keep returning to

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:20.140
which is the theory of
this, has to be right.

00:45:20.140 --> 00:45:22.673
But nobody has yet managed
to crack this at scale.

00:45:24.730 --> 00:45:25.563
- Thank you Rory.

00:45:25.563 --> 00:45:26.430
Any other thoughts on those questions?

00:45:26.430 --> 00:45:28.340
Otherwise we have plenty more coming in.

00:45:28.340 --> 00:45:30.533
I'm thrilled with our engagement here.

00:45:31.390 --> 00:45:33.622
I wanna to this question

00:45:33.622 --> 00:45:37.253
from again Sanjeev Kumar here.

00:45:38.140 --> 00:45:39.570
And I think it's very well put

00:45:39.570 --> 00:45:42.750
and I wonder if this is
something Sunil might speak to.

00:45:42.750 --> 00:45:46.690
Sanjeev asks my sense is that
the philosophical foundation

00:45:46.690 --> 00:45:49.810
undergirding the work of
researchers and policy makers

00:45:49.810 --> 00:45:52.340
is too narrow to empathize with the pain

00:45:52.340 --> 00:45:54.390
the current course of
development and growth

00:45:54.390 --> 00:45:56.010
is inflicting on people.

00:45:56.010 --> 00:45:58.310
Do you think that we
need a deep re-evaluation

00:45:58.310 --> 00:46:00.763
of the way we build
knowledge about the world?

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:03.893
- That is beautifully put.

00:46:04.891 --> 00:46:07.341
And I think in fact sort of epitomizes

00:46:07.341 --> 00:46:10.680
some of the what we're grappling with now.

00:46:10.680 --> 00:46:15.230
Because in a sense, there
have long been multiple ways

00:46:15.230 --> 00:46:16.373
of knowing the world,

00:46:17.210 --> 00:46:19.310
fully capable of comprehending the pain

00:46:19.310 --> 00:46:22.030
that some of the current parts
of development have inflicted

00:46:22.030 --> 00:46:24.169
on people around the world.

00:46:24.169 --> 00:46:26.060
Those are not the kinds of knowledge

00:46:26.060 --> 00:46:29.300
that have tended to be
valued or influential

00:46:29.300 --> 00:46:31.235
within the development sector,

00:46:31.235 --> 00:46:34.060
or within a lot of policymaking.

00:46:34.060 --> 00:46:38.380
So that is a gap that I think
has widened since the 1980s,

00:46:38.380 --> 00:46:40.980
where there is a lot of
work by anthropologists

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:41.930
like James Ferguson

00:46:41.930 --> 00:46:44.760
and others writing the
late 80s, early 90s.

00:46:44.760 --> 00:46:46.240
He wrote a wonderful book in 1990

00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:48.280
called the " Anti-Politics Machine"

00:46:48.280 --> 00:46:50.700
about the sort of deep politicizing

00:46:50.700 --> 00:46:52.850
thrust of a lot of development.

00:46:52.850 --> 00:46:55.280
He's also worked in Southern
Africa like Julie at the time

00:46:55.280 --> 00:46:58.810
and it doesn't seem to me that that debate

00:46:58.810 --> 00:47:00.780
has really sort of intersected

00:47:00.780 --> 00:47:02.010
with some of the conversations

00:47:02.010 --> 00:47:04.640
that are being had within
the development sector

00:47:04.640 --> 00:47:07.640
about metrics, about measuring success,

00:47:07.640 --> 00:47:09.340
the kinds of points that
that Rory was making.

00:47:09.340 --> 00:47:11.820
How does different measure success?

00:47:11.820 --> 00:47:15.900
It may well be
anthropologists are involved

00:47:15.900 --> 00:47:18.160
with the World Bank and are being employed

00:47:18.160 --> 00:47:20.456
by some of the international
development agencies,

00:47:20.456 --> 00:47:23.890
but it's not the same as to
say that some of those forms

00:47:23.890 --> 00:47:28.300
of knowledge, have truly
sort of started to inform

00:47:28.300 --> 00:47:30.267
or influence the way in which
we think about development.

00:47:30.267 --> 00:47:34.930
And I mean I do like very much
the way that Sanjeev's use

00:47:34.930 --> 00:47:36.930
of the word pain.

00:47:36.930 --> 00:47:38.740
And I mean I think that is important.

00:47:38.740 --> 00:47:40.960
These are very emotive topics.

00:47:40.960 --> 00:47:43.500
One only needs to look
at the farmers protests

00:47:43.500 --> 00:47:44.550
going on in India now

00:47:44.550 --> 00:47:47.300
that sort of enormous
colossal scale to understand

00:47:47.300 --> 00:47:48.920
that something has gone wrong.

00:47:48.920 --> 00:47:51.210
In the sense that Punjab

00:47:51.210 --> 00:47:54.250
was India's agricultural success story,

00:47:54.250 --> 00:47:55.889
since the green revolution

00:47:55.889 --> 00:47:59.932
and clearly that masks so much inequality

00:47:59.932 --> 00:48:02.950
and so much that is now manifesting itself

00:48:02.950 --> 00:48:05.253
in really a major political movement.

00:48:06.260 --> 00:48:07.980
- And Sunil you mentioned
the farmer's protest.

00:48:07.980 --> 00:48:11.510
That brings me to a question
from Camille Hassan,

00:48:11.510 --> 00:48:13.880
who asks about the role of labor.

00:48:13.880 --> 00:48:15.680
So I think we've talked a lot about,

00:48:16.810 --> 00:48:19.690
from a policy level, private sector,

00:48:19.690 --> 00:48:21.550
more top down in some of the framing

00:48:21.550 --> 00:48:23.290
in terms of actions to be taken.

00:48:23.290 --> 00:48:24.720
Now I'd love to talk a little bit more

00:48:24.720 --> 00:48:28.940
about in the grassroots
advocacy bottom up, if you will

00:48:28.940 --> 00:48:30.420
and not that I love that framing

00:48:30.420 --> 00:48:33.716
but here is the question
from Kamil Hassan.

00:48:33.716 --> 00:48:38.250
I'd like to perhaps push onto
the urgency of this question

00:48:38.250 --> 00:48:40.040
the way Sunil began.

00:48:40.040 --> 00:48:43.560
What is the position of labor,
in the contemporary moment?

00:48:43.560 --> 00:48:47.850
And Kamil asks about the US for example

00:48:47.850 --> 00:48:49.770
in the midst of a struggle
to raise the minimum wage,

00:48:49.770 --> 00:48:51.740
but how do we engage with labor

00:48:51.740 --> 00:48:53.550
with regards to the
conversation on growth?

00:48:53.550 --> 00:48:56.670
So any thoughts on that labor

00:48:56.670 --> 00:49:00.970
and then perhaps more broadly
just advocacy and demand

00:49:00.970 --> 00:49:05.440
from people most effected
by where we go from here?

00:49:05.440 --> 00:49:08.450
I see Julie might want to
jump in on this go ahead.

00:49:08.450 --> 00:49:12.740
- Yeah I will say that, in
Botswana where I do my research

00:49:12.740 --> 00:49:17.267
where you've had this really
spectacular economic growth,

00:49:17.267 --> 00:49:20.740
high unemployment has
remained in tractable.

00:49:20.740 --> 00:49:23.990
There is something happening
in our global economy

00:49:23.990 --> 00:49:28.990
which is the evisceration in
the slow disappearance of labor

00:49:29.780 --> 00:49:30.860
in many ways.

00:49:30.860 --> 00:49:33.230
And we see that manifest

00:49:33.230 --> 00:49:36.400
in the kind of political reconfiguration

00:49:36.400 --> 00:49:39.010
that you still lean on labor,

00:49:39.010 --> 00:49:42.977
as being the political entry point

00:49:42.977 --> 00:49:45.660
for certain sectors of society.

00:49:45.660 --> 00:49:49.253
And that's being, heavily,
heavily impinged on.

00:49:50.510 --> 00:49:54.700
I'm glad that the person
writing the question

00:49:54.700 --> 00:49:56.100
was pointing to the US

00:49:56.100 --> 00:49:58.230
where we see that around the minimum wage.

00:49:58.230 --> 00:50:01.650
And we see it around tensions
over union organizing

00:50:01.650 --> 00:50:03.253
and essential workers right now.

00:50:03.253 --> 00:50:07.390
I think that can be generalized globally.

00:50:07.390 --> 00:50:09.970
And I do think that it is one of the ways

00:50:09.970 --> 00:50:13.799
that the basic income grant
is just incredibly important.

00:50:13.799 --> 00:50:16.840
Because many people in
this world have no choice

00:50:16.840 --> 00:50:21.840
but to sell their labor at a price value,

00:50:22.380 --> 00:50:26.338
that is absolutely unacceptable,

00:50:26.338 --> 00:50:31.338
or to turn themselves
essentially into a debt,

00:50:32.520 --> 00:50:36.240
in order to be able to transit somewhere,

00:50:36.240 --> 00:50:37.752
to be able to be employed.

00:50:37.752 --> 00:50:42.610
And that can't be the solution to poverty

00:50:42.610 --> 00:50:43.793
or the environment.

00:50:45.890 --> 00:50:48.570
- I wanna throw in, Rory did
you wanna jump in on that?

00:50:48.570 --> 00:50:49.573
- If I can very quickly

00:50:49.573 --> 00:50:51.420
just to very strongly agree with Julie.

00:50:51.420 --> 00:50:53.547
I mean one of the most
heartbreaking things

00:50:53.547 --> 00:50:57.240
in many many Sub-Saharan
African countries in particular,

00:50:57.240 --> 00:50:58.330
is the unemployment rates.

00:50:58.330 --> 00:51:00.090
I mean around the edges of Dar es Salaam

00:51:00.090 --> 00:51:01.230
you've got unemployment rates

00:51:01.230 --> 00:51:04.192
amongst young people of about 85%.

00:51:04.192 --> 00:51:06.610
And the population

00:51:06.610 --> 00:51:08.360
of course is growing unbelievably quickly.

00:51:08.360 --> 00:51:10.180
So that by 2050 it's projected

00:51:10.180 --> 00:51:11.603
that one in 10 children born in the world

00:51:11.603 --> 00:51:13.970
will be born in Nigeria.

00:51:13.970 --> 00:51:17.520
But the hope which is that
you could combine capital

00:51:17.520 --> 00:51:19.573
from the developed world with the labor

00:51:19.573 --> 00:51:22.280
from rapidly growing places like Africa

00:51:22.280 --> 00:51:23.690
just hasn't happened.

00:51:23.690 --> 00:51:25.800
And indeed changes in technology,

00:51:25.800 --> 00:51:30.010
robotics, AI et cetera,
suggest it may not happen.

00:51:30.010 --> 00:51:32.870
And so, it's actually very miserable.

00:51:32.870 --> 00:51:35.520
I mean I get very sad
when I get about Botswana.

00:51:35.520 --> 00:51:38.621
Visiting communities which
as Julie says theoretically

00:51:38.621 --> 00:51:40.720
moving into an upper middle income status

00:51:40.720 --> 00:51:42.913
but where everybody is basically,

00:51:43.910 --> 00:51:45.723
is struggling to feel at ease.

00:51:46.710 --> 00:51:48.840
- Well, I would say that I think Botswana

00:51:48.840 --> 00:51:52.637
is doing pretty well for the
majority of the population.

00:51:52.637 --> 00:51:55.420
But that said, these are economies

00:51:55.420 --> 00:51:57.463
that are based around extraction.

00:51:58.420 --> 00:52:00.530
And that is sort of the
elephant in the room

00:52:00.530 --> 00:52:02.730
that we haven't really talked about.

00:52:02.730 --> 00:52:04.210
They're extractive

00:52:04.210 --> 00:52:06.180
and that's meant to provide the employment

00:52:06.180 --> 00:52:08.124
and then the purchase,

00:52:08.124 --> 00:52:12.923
in a global marketplace that
doesn't localize production,

00:52:13.940 --> 00:52:15.520
you know I don't know.

00:52:15.520 --> 00:52:19.400
I feel like the WTO is
somehow get another elephant

00:52:19.400 --> 00:52:21.513
in this room, that maybe we haven't.

00:52:23.780 --> 00:52:24.690
- That's a really interesting point

00:52:24.690 --> 00:52:25.820
and it relates to one more question

00:52:25.820 --> 00:52:27.754
I wanna bring in from the
audience as we start to wrap up.

00:52:27.754 --> 00:52:30.150
Because it's forward-looking.

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:32.468
So this is from and I hope I'm
saying your name correctly,

00:52:32.468 --> 00:52:36.290
Morrison Bluhm, who asks
how can the global North

00:52:36.290 --> 00:52:38.810
transform the conversation
from carbon emissions

00:52:38.810 --> 00:52:42.340
to inequality, as well as the
global North's own history

00:52:42.340 --> 00:52:43.500
of over-consumption?

00:52:43.500 --> 00:52:46.850
So I don't know how optimistic
we are that that can happen

00:52:46.850 --> 00:52:49.020
but what would that look like?

00:52:49.020 --> 00:52:50.330
And how might it happen?

00:52:50.330 --> 00:52:51.930
Any thoughts from our panelists?

00:52:54.240 --> 00:52:55.350
- So I think one,

00:52:55.350 --> 00:52:57.780
I mean just link this
back to the last question

00:52:57.780 --> 00:52:59.430
on thinking about labor movements.

00:52:59.430 --> 00:53:00.838
So (audio glitches) we think progress

00:53:00.838 --> 00:53:04.160
and we think about these
kind of broad discussions

00:53:04.160 --> 00:53:05.690
about the green new deal

00:53:05.690 --> 00:53:08.320
or the linking of things
like, the minimum wage

00:53:08.320 --> 00:53:12.430
to saying that we want to
think about growth differently.

00:53:12.430 --> 00:53:14.760
That maybe one way forward.

00:53:14.760 --> 00:53:16.380
My concern does remain

00:53:16.380 --> 00:53:19.780
that these tend to be quite
domestic conversations.

00:53:19.780 --> 00:53:22.450
And so I think that another
place to think about

00:53:22.450 --> 00:53:25.440
which I feel we started
too one of the last one

00:53:25.440 --> 00:53:28.450
but not really is, how climate change

00:53:28.450 --> 00:53:31.200
and also the lack of kind of good jobs

00:53:31.200 --> 00:53:35.730
in the developing world
is, underlying a lot

00:53:35.730 --> 00:53:37.938
of what we see on the migration trends.

00:53:37.938 --> 00:53:40.230
So if you think a lot of what's happening

00:53:40.230 --> 00:53:42.090
on the kind of the cold, severe up,

00:53:42.090 --> 00:53:46.120
or from Central America to the US,

00:53:46.120 --> 00:53:48.530
has its deeper roots in
sort of climate change

00:53:48.530 --> 00:53:51.870
combined with, people first
go to the urban centers

00:53:51.870 --> 00:53:54.847
in this country there aren't a
lot of jobs (audio glitches).

00:53:56.930 --> 00:53:58.978
I mean that's perhaps
also way that the world

00:53:58.978 --> 00:54:00.710
is going to get into LinkedIn.

00:54:00.710 --> 00:54:03.940
Maybe at some point we'll
stop having the nasty reaction

00:54:03.940 --> 00:54:05.340
that we can just keep people out

00:54:05.340 --> 00:54:07.431
and actually start
dealing a little bit more

00:54:07.431 --> 00:54:08.860
with the root causes.

00:54:08.860 --> 00:54:11.810
But I think labor is going to be critical

00:54:11.810 --> 00:54:13.710
to how we link these conversations

00:54:13.710 --> 00:54:15.433
across rich and poor countries.

00:54:16.510 --> 00:54:18.290
- Agreed. So we have just
five minutes left here

00:54:18.290 --> 00:54:20.030
and I wanna quickly hear from each of you

00:54:20.030 --> 00:54:22.002
if there's anything we didn't
get to in this conversation.

00:54:22.002 --> 00:54:24.170
I'll throw in one question I had

00:54:24.170 --> 00:54:27.003
that I'd love any thoughts on

00:54:27.003 --> 00:54:30.320
but we'll open it up to see
what you might wanna add.

00:54:30.320 --> 00:54:32.240
So I think one thing we
weren't able to get into

00:54:32.240 --> 00:54:33.780
as much as I would like is measurement.

00:54:33.780 --> 00:54:37.400
So we talked, we had a bit
of a debate around growth,

00:54:37.400 --> 00:54:40.240
de-growth, rethinking growth,

00:54:40.240 --> 00:54:43.400
but that has to do with
what metrics we use

00:54:43.400 --> 00:54:45.510
and I do think there is growing consensus

00:54:45.510 --> 00:54:48.140
that economic growth
is not the best metric

00:54:48.140 --> 00:54:50.690
to capture poverty
reduction, biodiversity,

00:54:50.690 --> 00:54:52.500
the wellbeing of people and the planet

00:54:52.500 --> 00:54:54.240
and how interconnected that is.

00:54:54.240 --> 00:54:56.921
So if this is a moment for a reset,

00:54:56.921 --> 00:54:59.580
how might we change the way we measure?

00:54:59.580 --> 00:55:00.800
So that's a question from me

00:55:00.800 --> 00:55:03.240
but perhaps panelists have
something else they'd like to say

00:55:03.240 --> 00:55:05.040
with just a minute each we'll go around.

00:55:05.040 --> 00:55:07.180
I'll actually start with
Rohini if that's all right.

00:55:07.180 --> 00:55:09.920
And maybe we can end with Sunil,

00:55:09.920 --> 00:55:10.753
where we began.

00:55:10.753 --> 00:55:12.100
So Rohini let's start with you.

00:55:12.100 --> 00:55:14.470
Final thoughts, and perhaps
it has to do with measurement

00:55:14.470 --> 00:55:16.270
or anything else you'd like to note.

00:55:19.320 --> 00:55:20.323
- Yeah so I'll start the measurement

00:55:20.323 --> 00:55:22.050
actually a different measurement

00:55:22.050 --> 00:55:23.210
than the one you talked about

00:55:23.210 --> 00:55:26.440
but one about measuring the
effects of climate policy.

00:55:26.440 --> 00:55:29.170
So I still think a case that
a lot of the discussions

00:55:29.170 --> 00:55:31.040
on how climate change policy will happen

00:55:31.040 --> 00:55:34.100
is being undertaken by lawyers.

00:55:34.100 --> 00:55:35.663
So a lot of these conversations

00:55:35.663 --> 00:55:38.608
we know happen among kind of in,

00:55:38.608 --> 00:55:41.300
it could be COVID-26 coming up

00:55:41.300 --> 00:55:43.350
otherwise it will be a lot of lawyers

00:55:43.350 --> 00:55:45.210
talking about work done

00:55:45.210 --> 00:55:46.927
or what are the
responsibilities of countries.

00:55:46.927 --> 00:55:49.040
Without thinking about implementation

00:55:49.040 --> 00:55:50.720
of how you would measure it.

00:55:50.720 --> 00:55:54.480
And I think we repeatedly
see a lot of the failures

00:55:54.480 --> 00:55:56.930
of international climate change policy,

00:55:56.930 --> 00:55:59.560
coming from not thinking
about how we measure them.

00:55:59.560 --> 00:56:03.233
I think a classic example
for instances is offsets.

00:56:03.233 --> 00:56:05.370
Offsets have become very popular

00:56:05.370 --> 00:56:07.190
as a way of thinking about how you would,

00:56:07.190 --> 00:56:09.320
do your little bit for climate change.

00:56:09.320 --> 00:56:11.490
But how do we know what we're offsetting?

00:56:11.490 --> 00:56:13.020
Would that action even have happened?

00:56:13.020 --> 00:56:15.620
I mean that goes to the
heart of what I think,

00:56:15.620 --> 00:56:17.290
economists measure all the time,

00:56:17.290 --> 00:56:18.940
which is what was the count of factory,

00:56:18.940 --> 00:56:20.980
what was going to happen
without the offset.

00:56:20.980 --> 00:56:24.590
And I think if that room
didn't just have lawyers

00:56:24.590 --> 00:56:26.880
and people sort of thinking
about what is acceptable

00:56:26.880 --> 00:56:27.960
to the private sector,

00:56:27.960 --> 00:56:30.480
but actually thought about
measurement and impacts,

00:56:30.480 --> 00:56:33.830
maybe on this policy we would
also make a bit more progress.

00:56:33.830 --> 00:56:34.730
So that's from me.

00:56:35.590 --> 00:56:36.423
- Thanks Rohini.

00:56:36.423 --> 00:56:38.230
Rory we'll go to you next.

00:56:38.230 --> 00:56:39.393
- So for me on measurement,

00:56:39.393 --> 00:56:41.330
I think it's really important

00:56:41.330 --> 00:56:44.250
to look at a measurement
and natural capital

00:56:44.250 --> 00:56:46.650
which is very central
to the policy debates

00:56:46.650 --> 00:56:48.030
certainly in the UK.

00:56:48.030 --> 00:56:49.610
But actually increasing the elsewhere

00:56:49.610 --> 00:56:53.520
in terms of trying to work
out how you attach a value

00:56:53.520 --> 00:56:56.638
to carbon, climate, biodiversity,

00:56:56.638 --> 00:56:59.643
and people's interactions
with natural landscapes.

00:57:00.860 --> 00:57:03.430
It is unbelievably difficult,

00:57:03.430 --> 00:57:07.350
to get any sort of agreement around it.

00:57:07.350 --> 00:57:11.120
Because small differences in how much

00:57:11.120 --> 00:57:12.850
value you attribute for example

00:57:12.850 --> 00:57:15.940
to measure or an aesthetic appreciation

00:57:15.940 --> 00:57:18.360
can completely transform
the value you ascribe

00:57:18.360 --> 00:57:20.483
to a particular landscape.

00:57:21.656 --> 00:57:25.760
It's very, very difficult
to explain to the public,

00:57:25.760 --> 00:57:30.020
it's something that is
in a dangerous situation

00:57:30.020 --> 00:57:33.300
because it's something
where, we have a strong

00:57:33.300 --> 00:57:35.740
emotional and idealistic
attachment to the idea

00:57:35.740 --> 00:57:37.590
of natural capital measurement,

00:57:37.590 --> 00:57:40.160
and that therefore
suppressing some very good

00:57:40.160 --> 00:57:42.360
and critical questions about
how these models operate

00:57:42.360 --> 00:57:43.790
and whether they're really effective ways

00:57:43.790 --> 00:57:45.270
of allocating resources.

00:57:45.270 --> 00:57:47.880
Getting that right is gonna
be a very, very important part

00:57:47.880 --> 00:57:50.000
of determining particularly
how governments

00:57:50.000 --> 00:57:51.570
choose to spend their money.

00:57:51.570 --> 00:57:55.010
How treasuries assess investments
in these kinds of things

00:57:55.010 --> 00:57:56.910
both domestically and internationally.

00:57:58.190 --> 00:58:00.430
- And Julie thoughts from
you on how we measure

00:58:00.430 --> 00:58:02.380
or any other closing thoughts you have.

00:58:04.190 --> 00:58:05.520
Oh you're on mute.

00:58:05.520 --> 00:58:06.353
There you go.

00:58:06.353 --> 00:58:07.641
- You think after a year of this

00:58:07.641 --> 00:58:10.039
I haven't learned about that?

00:58:10.039 --> 00:58:13.870
I think moving away from
GDP as the measurement

00:58:13.870 --> 00:58:15.845
is really important.

00:58:15.845 --> 00:58:18.640
As a public health
person I know for example

00:58:18.640 --> 00:58:21.690
the GDP is underneath all
kinds of health indicators

00:58:21.690 --> 00:58:23.202
and how they're modeled.

00:58:23.202 --> 00:58:25.680
So for example when GDP goes up

00:58:25.680 --> 00:58:28.040
the maternal mortality rate goes down,

00:58:28.040 --> 00:58:32.263
whether or not there are
women dying in labor.

00:58:32.263 --> 00:58:36.480
So I think it's a blunt
measurement that needs to go away.

00:58:36.480 --> 00:58:39.460
And I'm a huge proponent of the work

00:58:39.460 --> 00:58:42.550
of the development economist Kate Raworth.

00:58:42.550 --> 00:58:47.043
R-A-W-O-R-T-H, and I recommend
you all read her book,

00:58:47.043 --> 00:58:49.970
" Doughnut economics" which
puts forward a different model

00:58:49.970 --> 00:58:50.803
of measurement.

00:58:52.140 --> 00:58:52.973
- Thank you Julie.

00:58:52.973 --> 00:58:54.930
I love when I come out of
conversations with action items.

00:58:54.930 --> 00:58:55.940
So I appreciate that.

00:58:55.940 --> 00:58:56.773
And we'll close with Sunil.

00:58:56.773 --> 00:58:58.873
Sunil I think this has been
a fantastic conversation.

00:58:58.873 --> 00:59:02.480
I appreciate you being the
one to suggest we have it.

00:59:02.480 --> 00:59:03.610
What are your closing thoughts

00:59:03.610 --> 00:59:05.800
or any calls to action for this group?

00:59:05.800 --> 00:59:07.370
- My closing thoughts
is not about measurement

00:59:07.370 --> 00:59:09.440
it's that we haven't said
anything about China.

00:59:09.440 --> 00:59:11.700
And I think if we are going
to think about the future

00:59:11.700 --> 00:59:13.366
of growth and de-growth,

00:59:13.366 --> 00:59:16.480
we probably need to engage
with what those might mean

00:59:16.480 --> 00:59:19.010
in the context of the
belt and road initiative

00:59:19.010 --> 00:59:22.200
in the context of China's
ambitions across Eurasia

00:59:22.200 --> 00:59:23.830
and indeed into Africa.

00:59:23.830 --> 00:59:25.940
And so I would just
sort of end with a call

00:59:25.940 --> 00:59:28.205
to really take a global
perspective on this,

00:59:28.205 --> 00:59:30.173
perhaps even moving
between the global North

00:59:30.173 --> 00:59:32.780
and the global South in
the traditional towns

00:59:32.780 --> 00:59:34.130
we think about development.

00:59:35.050 --> 00:59:35.883
- Thank you Sunil.

00:59:35.883 --> 00:59:38.150
Perhaps the starting
point for a future edition

00:59:38.150 --> 00:59:39.162
of the Yale Development Dialogues.

00:59:39.162 --> 00:59:41.720
Well I wanna make sure we end on time.

00:59:41.720 --> 00:59:43.970
Thank you all so much
for the great questions,

00:59:43.970 --> 00:59:45.480
and we'll make sure to get back to those

00:59:45.480 --> 00:59:47.240
we didn't have time to answer.

00:59:47.240 --> 00:59:48.370
Thank you to our panelists

00:59:48.370 --> 00:59:51.230
and stay tuned for upcoming additions

00:59:51.230 --> 00:59:52.290
of the Yale Development Dialogues

00:59:52.290 --> 00:59:55.270
where I think as we've all
just seen the value of here

00:59:55.270 --> 00:59:58.340
we bring historians and
practitioners together

00:59:58.340 --> 01:00:00.133
to discuss some of these
very complicated issues

01:00:00.133 --> 01:00:01.470
and the way forward.

01:00:01.470 --> 01:00:02.303
Thank you all.

